---
title: "Widget-shape diagnostics"
---

This document deliberately builds every interactive container **wrong**, so the widget
validators in `render/validate.rs` have a fixture that actually trips them. The real corpus
only ever builds these correctly, which is exactly why their diagnostics fell through
`codes::classify` to the uncatalogued code at ERROR severity for as long as they did. It
lives in `corpus/diagnostics/`, which is exempt from the corpus clean-render guards.

## A tabset with no tabs

A `.panel-tabset` gets its tabs from the `##` headings inside it. With none, it renders its
content as one undifferentiated blob and no tab strip at all.

::: {.panel-tabset}
Prose, but no headings, so there is nothing to build a tab from.
:::

## A walkthrough with nothing to walk through

A `.code-walkthrough` pins a code block in a sticky panel and drives its line focus from the
steps beside it. With no code block the sticky panel is empty.

::: {.code-walkthrough}
::: {.step}
A step with no code block to focus.
:::
:::

## A scrolly missing each of its halves

A `.scrolly` needs both a sticky stage (a figure or a `{js}` cell) and `.step` divs to
scroll past it. Each half is missing once, because the two are separate diagnostics: any
non-`.step` block counts as the stage, so one container cannot omit both.

::: {.scrolly}
::: {.step}
A step with nothing pinned beside it to drive.
:::
:::

::: {.scrolly}
A stage with nothing scrolling past it.
:::

## Reactive controls that cannot feed the graph

An anonymous control has no reactive identity, so nothing can read it:

{{< input type="number" value="1" label="anonymous" >}}

An unknown type has no control to build:

{{< input name="w" type="slidr" value="1" label="w" >}}

And a `select` with no options renders an empty menu:

{{< input name="c" type="select" label="channel" >}}

## A `.debug` with no traced cell

`::: {.debug}` needs exactly one cell marked `#| trace: true` (or `//| trace: true` for a
`{js}` generator) to know what to step through. Without one, the panel shows the code but
never becomes a stepper.

::: {.debug}
```{python}
a = [3, 1, 2]
```
:::

## A `.debug` with more than one traced cell

Only the first traced cell in a `.debug` div is ever stepped; a second one is silently
ignored otherwise, so this warns rather than picking one without saying so.

::: {.debug}
```{python}
#| trace: true
a = [3, 1, 2]
```

```{python}
#| trace: true
b = [4, 5, 6]
```
:::

## `#| trace: true` outside a `.debug` div

`trace:` only has an effect inside a `.debug` div, where its recorded steps become the
panel's step data. Elsewhere, the cell still runs and its full trace still gets recorded
and shipped as a dead `<script>` blob no widget will ever read: real work, spent for
nothing.

```{python}
#| trace: true
a = [3, 1, 2]
```

## A non-boolean `trace:` value

`trace:` only recognizes `true` and `false`. Any other value falls through the same
literal match that decides whether to run the tracer at all, so a typo like `yes` quietly
renders a plain, unstepped code block instead of the stepper the author asked for.

::: {.debug}
```{python}
#| trace: yes
a = [3, 1, 2]
```
:::
